---
import LayoutA from "../../layouts/LayoutA.astro";
import { Stack } from "../../components/ui";

const title = "Rent a design engineer vs hiring • Amoeba";
const description =
	"Renting a design engineer and hiring one full time solve different problems. What a weekly UI backlog gets you, and when a full-time seat is the better call.";
const pageUrl = "https://amoeba.design/compare/rent-a-design-engineer-vs-hiring";

const jsonLd = [
	{
		"@context": "https://schema.org",
		"@type": "Article",
		headline: "Rent a design engineer vs hiring",
		description,
		inLanguage: "en",
		datePublished: "2026-10-10",
		dateModified: "2026-10-10",
		author: {
			"@type": "Person",
			name: "Bryan King",
			url: "https://x.com/bryan_king",
		},
		publisher: {
			"@type": "Organization",
			name: "Amoeba",
			url: "https://amoeba.design",
		},
		mainEntityOfPage: pageUrl,
	},
	{
		"@context": "https://schema.org",
		"@type": "BreadcrumbList",
		itemListElement: [
			{ "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://amoeba.design/" },
			{ "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Rent a design engineer vs hiring", item: pageUrl },
		],
	},
];
---

<LayoutA title={title} description={description} ogType="article" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
	<Stack space="8" class="copy">
		<Stack space="4">
			<h1 class="type-title-alt">Rent a design engineer vs hiring</h1>
			<p>
				A full-time hire and a priced week of design engineering are different tools. People shop them like they
				are the same SKU. They solve different shapes of problem, and pretending otherwise is how you open a req
				for a backlog, or rent a week for a job that needed a teammate.
			</p>
			<p>
				I run <a href="/">Amoeba</a>, a design engineering studio for ambitious B2B software companies. The offer
				I publish is <a href="/rent-a-design-engineer">rent a design engineer</a>. The price on that page, and
				on the homepage, is $500 / week. This page is the tradeoff. I am not going to invent a salary on the
				other side of it.
			</p>
		</Stack>

		<Stack space="4">
			<h2>What the week actually is</h2>
			<p>
				You populate a backlog of UI issues. I ship polished UI as pull requests for your engineering team to
				review. SaaS product UI. React interfaces. Diffs in your repo. Your engineers review them. That is the
				whole mechanism. Backlog in. Pull requests out.
			</p>
			<p>
				The published price is $500 / week. The week is pointed. It is good at rough edges, at a design system
				the product has drifted off of, and at
				<a href="/knowledge/fix-ai-slop-ui">AI slop in the interface</a>. The work arrives as a pull request in
				your repo. Your team decides what merges. When the list is done, or when the next list is a different
				kind of problem, the week has done what it was for.
			</p>
		</Stack>

		<Stack space="4">
			<h2>What a full-time hire is for</h2>
			<p>
				A full-time design engineer, product designer, or frontend engineer stays. Context compounds. They learn
				the domain, the customers, the weird edge in the billing model, the reason a screen looks strange to
				everyone except the person who has been in the calls. They can follow your methodology by the book,
				which is what I want a team to do anyway. The best teams I have worked with live and die by that book.
			</p>
			<p>
				You pay a salary, benefits, and the time to recruit and manage. I will not quote a market rate. Posts
				about "design engineer" rates are full of mechanical and CAD contracting, which is a different trade,
				and a software number I grabbed from a blog would be theater. Talk to whoever is actually hiring in
				your market this quarter. The qualitative cost is enough to plan with: a seat is a commitment, a search
				takes longer than a week, and a bad fit is expensive in time even before the comp.
			</p>
			<p>
				The seat is the right shape when UI is a permanent function. Someone has to hold the design system over
				years, sit with customers, and keep the product coherent after the tenth feature. A rented week does
				not become that person. If you need the judgment in the building, hire.
			</p>
		</Stack>

		<Stack space="4">
			<h2>The trade, without a fake spreadsheet</h2>
			<p>
				Renting spends money on a defined list. Hiring spends money on a person who can find the next list.
				Both are rational. They fail in different ways.
			</p>
			<p>
				The failure mode of the week is a vague backlog. If you have not listened to customers, I will polish
				whatever you wrote down. Your customers will tell you what they want, and a week against a guessed
				list is a fast way to ship the wrong shine. The failure mode of the hire is a long search, a calendar
				full of metawork, and a skilled person spending the quarter in meetings about the interface instead of
				in the interface. Loaded calendars are how software companies waste the seat they fought to open.
			</p>
			<p>
				I think the useful unit of interface work is closer to a finished day than to a mythical man-month. That
				is an argument for small batches and limited work in progress. It is also an argument for hiring, when
				the batches never stop. A permanent backlog of UI, research, and system care is a job. A pile of
				concrete issues with a team that can review code is a week.
			</p>
			<p>
				There is a skill point underneath both options. The design engineer I mean can design and implement.
				<a href="/compare/design-engineer-vs-product-designer-vs-frontend-engineer">The three roles</a>
				are not interchangeable, and
				<a href="/knowledge/more-than-microinteractions">the definition</a>
				is worth reading before you write the req. Hiring a product designer to land pull requests, or a
				frontend engineer to discover the product, is how the seat disappoints you. Renting me to invent your
				roadmap would disappoint you too. I want a list.
			</p>
		</Stack>

		<Stack space="4">
			<h2>When I would do which</h2>
			<p>
				I would hire full time when the interface is a standing function, you have a clear job, and you want
				the person in the company. Research, the system, the politics of a screen, the tenth feature: that work
				compounds, and a teammate is who holds it.
			</p>
			<p>
				I would rent the week when the backlog is real and written down, your engineers can review pull
				requests, and what you need is polished UI in the product. Rough edges. Overrides. A React screen that
				looks generated. Run the <a href="/tools/ui-polish-checklist">UI polish checklist</a> if you want the
				issues to be specific before you send them. Then the offer is the plain one:
				<a href="/rent-a-design-engineer">$500 / week, backlog in, pull requests out</a>.
			</p>
			<p>
				You can do both, in that order or the other way around. A week can clear the debt before a new hire
				walks into it. A hire can own the product, and a week can cover a spike they should not context-switch
				into. I have not published a package that bundles those. They are separate decisions.
			</p>
			<p>Hire the person when the job is a job. Rent the week when the job is a backlog.</p>
		</Stack>
	</Stack>
</LayoutA>

<style>
	.copy {
		max-inline-size: var(--measure);
	}

	h1 {
		letter-spacing: var(--tracking-tight);
	}
</style>
